¶
'''wrinkle<sup>1</sup>''' (ring′kl), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''wrinkled'', ppr. ''wrinkling''. [= MD. ''wrinckelen'', ''wrynckelen''; from the noun.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To form wrinkles in; contract, fold, or pucker into small ridges and furrows or creases; corrugate; crease.
{{smaller block|Hollow eye and ''wrinkled'' brow.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. of V., iv. 1. 270.}}
<poem>Within the surface of the fleeting river
The ''wrinkled'' image of the city lay.</poem>
{{right|''Shelley'', Evening.}}
So yellow as she was, so ''wrinkled'', so sad of mien!
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Seven Gables, vii.}}
No care may ''wrinkle'' thy smooth brow.
{{right|''William Morris'', Earthly Paradise, I. 157.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To become contracted into wrinkles; shrink into furrows and ridges; be marked with wrinkles.
{{smaller block|<poem>When high in the field the fern-leaves ''wrinkle'',
And brown is the grass where the mowers have mown.</poem>
{{right|''R. W. Gilder'', Lyrics, Song of Early Autumn.}}
Mrs. Putney was a small woman, already beginning to ''wrinkle''.
{{right|''Howells'', Annie Kilburn, iv.}}}}

