readings.c1s3:
slang, of ''wrinkle''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'' According to Skeat, it is a dim. of ME. ''wrink'', ''wrenk'', < AS. ''wrenc'', a trick: see ''wrench'', ''n.''] A short pithy piece of information or advice; a valuable hint; a bit of useful knowledge or instruction; a good idea; a trick; a point; a notion; a device. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|They are too experte in loue, hauing learned in this time of their long peace euery ''wrinckle'' that is to be seene or imagined. {{right|''Lyly'', Euphues and his England, p. 389.}}
Philip, when thou goes courtin’, come t’ me, and a’ll give thee many a ''wrinkle''. {{right|''Mrs. Gaskell'', Sylvia’s Lovers, xii.}}
Oh, you are up to this ''wrinkle'', are you? {{right|''Harper’s Mag.'', LXXVIII. 559.}}}}

'''wrinkle-beaked''' (ring′kl-bēkt), ''a.'' Having a wrinkled, sulcate, or ridged and furrowed bill: specifying one of the anis, ''Crotophaga sulcirostris.'' {{smaller|This bird is common in parts of Texas, and thence through much of South America. See cut under ''ani''.}}

'''wrinkled''' (ring′kld), ''a.'' In ''zoöl.'', marked with parallel and somewhat irregular raised lines; having wrinkles; rugose; corrugated.—'''Wrinkled hornbill,''' {{smaller|the bird ''Cranorhinus corrugatus'', whose high carinated casque is laterally corrugated.}}

'''wrinkling-machine''' (ringk′ling-ma-shēn″), ''n.'' A machine for forming transverse wrinkles on the upper leathers of boots and shoes.

'''wrinkly''' (ringk′li), ''a.'' [< ''wrinkle''<sup>1</sup> + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] Somewhat wrinkled; having a tendency to be wrinkled; puckered; creased.

{{smaller block|His old ''wrinkly'' face grew quite blown-out at last. {{right|''Carlyle'', The Century, XXIV. 18.}}
Mrs. Waule . . . giving occasional dry ''wrinkly'' indications of crying. {{right|''George Eliot'', Middlemarch, xxxii.}}}}

