{{smaller|several metacarpals, together with the ligaments and other associated soft parts, are included in the term ''wrist''. The motions of the wrist as a whole upon the forearm include all the movements of flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, and circumduction, together with the movements of pronation and supination impressed upon the wrist by the rocking of the radius about the ulna; but the motion of the individual carpal bones upon one another is slight, and that between the distal carpals and the metacarpals is still less. In most other animals than man, the movements of the wrist are more restricted. The term is extended to the corresponding joint of the fore limb of other mammals, birds, and reptiles. Thus the so-called knee of the horse’s fore leg is anatomically the carpus or wrist. See ''carpus'', and cuts under ''hand'', ''pisiform'', and ''scapholunar''.}}
{{smaller block|Little Preston was found there with both his hands cut off by the ''wreasts''.
{{right|''W. Patten'', Ex. into Scotland (Arber’s Eng. Garner, III. 128).}}}}
'''2†.''' The ankle or the instep.
{{smaller block|<poem>Then he put on the old man’s hose,
Were patch’d from knee to ''wrist''.</poem>
{{right|''Robin Hood Rescuing the Widow’s Three Sons'' (Child’s Ballads, V. 264).}}}}
'''3.''' In ''mach.'', a stud or pin projecting from the side of a crank, wheel, or other moving part, and forming a means of attachment to a connecting-rod leading to some other part of the mechanism. Also called ''wrist-pin''.{{smaller|—'''Bridle wrist,''' in the ''manège'', the wrist of the horseman’s left hand. Compare ''bridle-hand''.—'''Twist of the wrist.''' See ''twist''.—'''Wrist touch,''' in ''pianoforte-playing'', a stroke or touch which proceeds from the wrist rather than from the fingers alone or from the whole forearm.}}

